it's 5:00 a.m. and we're on our way to  Guanabara a suburb of the Cuban capital  Havana  Joell Montero Herrera and his wife unit  sir are rebuilding their home that's not  the only big change here six months ago  Joelle started his own one-man taxi  business it's now six o'clock  what's your oil doing up so early we  asked actually says he's off to a late  start he's broken a headlight normally  he'd be on the road by 4:00 a.m. while  he's waiting for the Sun to come up you  need to make some some more strong  coffee of course I'd rather he stay home  she says but he has to work I'm pregnant  and I can't work so he's got to manage  for us all before their five-year-old  son is even out of bed Joelle tells you  need some good bye it's time for work  joella's taxi a 1955 Chevrolet classic  the cab cost him the equivalent of  11,000 euros Joelle borrowed the money  from his father-in-law there aren't that  many taxis out yet he tells us it's  gonna be a good day and soon enough he's  already picked up his first passengers  heading for town Joelle's new enterprise  is only possible because of economic  reforms in Cuba that have made it  possible for small private businesses to  sprout up  what will Cuba be like in five years we  ask no idea he says and in a decade  absolutely no idea he says Joelle tells  us the world is changing not just in  Cuba across the world things are  developing and there are also some  things that are getting worse  he says here in our country things are  no different there's a lot of  uncertainty no one knows what could  happen tomorrow hopefully he says our  exchange rates will improve oil explains  that Cuba has two currencies the Cuban  peso used every day by the vast majority  of Cubans and the convertible peso used  often by foreign tourists to buy luxury  goods he says on a good day he can make  1,100 regular pesos but figure in fuel  costs in the exchange rate and he's down  to ten convertible pesos and profits  that's about ten US dollars which means  to make a living  Joell drives about fourteen hours a day  six days a week still he's proud of his  work and his car  it's a rolling Museum in other countries  these cars are seen as a copy as class  except people keep for their own  pleasure here it's our means of  transport our work and our income  thanks to these old American cars we  have work the cars are sturdy they're  the only ones that would last this long  Michael wanted  we leave Joella Havana for now and head  off 200 kilometers to the west to Pinar  del río province the world's most famous  tobacco region we're here to visit  Francisco Jose Prieto and his father  Pancho they are members of a farmers  collective their land has been in the  family for generations Francisco Jose  shows us where the leaves are hung to  dry he says the system makes more space  for the women to do their work  it is mostly women who cure the tobacco  leaves most of them have been added for  many years their work is especially  needed during the high season from  January to March once the leaves are  cured  the Cuban state will buy up the entire  harvest and recently the state has  started paying more than it used to for  workers like 72 year-old Marita Alvarez  that means higher wages good news for  her and her sizeable family she says she  always hoped her children would go to  university and all three managed to do  it now all her grandchildren are at  university of course she's very happy  about that now everything here is better  she says it used to be good work and  work and all you'd get was a few cents  now she says you really see the results  of your labor  Mirta tells us she gets paid twice a  month the last time she gets 665 Cuban  pesos even though work had to be  canceled a few times because of rain you  couldn't make that much before Cuba  cigars are next to sugar the islands  most important export good employees  here say they like working for Francisco  Jose Prieto  many have come back again and again for  decades to help out at harvest time the  oldest of them is in his mid-70s  Prieto says his workers are happy  voluntarily that one before they see a  happy man is one who gets up in the  morning and wants to go to work and when  his work is over wants to go home  everything else depends on personal  happiness and a stable family life what  about you we asked I'm a happy man he  says create those estate is successful  and foreign tourists come here to welter  Abajo to see where Cuba's world-famous  cigars come from many of them cigar  aficionados themselves and many of them  from Germany tour guide tells us group  in German that these leaves are used for  pipe tobacco and cigarettes some of the  visitors have been to Cuba before well  that's why I come here every two years  because it's beautiful here people are  nice there's good music and good company  what about the tobacco we asked Cuban  tobacco is the best out there 99.9  percent of the time we only smoke Cuban  cigars they still bunch all the tobacco  leaves together with a kind of needle  like 100 years ago good the people have  honest work I hope that they're well  paid that there's a minimum wage we  asked another worker Gladys Diaz what  she has to say about that  I'm still young she says but I did get  to know capitalism for a few years  before things changed when I retired I  came back here and I'm doing quite well  here Francisco Jose pays as well we get  to take meal breaks they bring us coffee  and cold water to drink it's really good  no think I feel like I had a Largo Oh  Wally yeah no choc jacket okay good  one of us here on Francisco Jose tells  us Cuba is moving along very well even  if the changes have been slow in coming  he says they are very much for the  better he says you just have to go out  into the streets to see the differences  everything that's cropping up all the  new private businesses that people are  building every day there's something new  that's changed our lives  I can't be a little too long you know  you it really has changed it's gotten  much better one change that would be  hard for me though is if my son doesn't  take over the farm this is our lives  work you lunchtime it's a break best  enjoyed in the shade the workers wait  for the Sun to go down a bit more before  they get back to their posts  the highway back to Havana is nearly  empty in town at a dance show we meet up  again with taxi owner Joe L his wife who  Nitze and some friends of the couple  Joelle has even taken the afternoon off  the for ride in Joelle's Chevy along the  Malecon Havana's popular coastal route  vasin the rabble we come here to drink a  beer sit in the Sun and enjoy the cool  breeze he says to chat a little then  forget about our troubles and our work  for a little while you'll be unknown at  all what kind of troubles we ask joy  says he's worried about getting their  home rebuilt before the baby comes  then there's the car if anything breaks  it's a tough situation there are no  replacement parts available your needs  is thinking even further ahead maybe in  three or four years when we're better  off we can have another kid she says but  maybe not our living space is already  very cramped no more sea soil tells us  before buying his taxi he was an  economist for the Cuban state for seven  years and at the end he says what could  I do  oh now I'm self-employed so that I can  support myself and my family  the four go for a stroll through  Havana's old town popular among foreign  tourists  it's expensive here Joelle says how  expensive we asked well for us it's  super expensive he says if you take our  national peso and exchange it for the  convertible peso it's clear we can't  afford it here miss areas for foreign  tourists so why do they come here most  like to look around he says that's all  we can do why not  the friends are happy to be here even if  it's just for a little daydreaming  you
 

